Requirements
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
UllaGulla  has  been  written  and  tested using CNet/4. It will definitely
*NOT*  work on CNet/3 (which has no "GO" command). It has *not* been tested
on  CNet/5,  but unless the Getuser variable for the subboards 'go key' has
changed, it should.

Besides  that,  it  requires ARexx up and running, and some minutes to read
into this lengthy documentation. :)


Installation.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Load  your BBSTEXT into an editor and append to line #598 (this is the line
that originally says something like "you don't have upload access here"):

     #0<path>Ullagulla.rexx}

...where  <path>  is  the  directory  where  you  copied  UllaGulla and the
configuration to. As always, we in 1oo% suggest that you use something like
"Doors:1oo/" as destination. :) Yes, thats all.

NOTE:  there may be items "new" to the current user in the upload directory
(where  the users will be put to). In that case, CNet then asks the user if
he  wants  to read/scan/browse the new items or not. Since I can't check if
there  ARE  new  items  or  not the easy way, I add a a simple CR (carriage
return)  to  the command, which is, in case the prompt appears, the default
CNet answer "NO".


Configuration
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
If  you want to prompt your users for the jump to the upload directory, set
the  "ASK"  variable  somewhere  at  the beginning of the script to "1". If
UllaGulla  should  take  them directly to the upload subboard and start the
upload, set it to "0" (without the quote marks, of course).

Now, to the configfile ("UllaGulla.cfg").

It  is recommended that you have a somewhat logic setup of go keywords that
reflect  the  pathes  of  your  subboard  structure.  In  my BBS, all Amiga
subboards have go keys like "Ami/Demo", "Ami/Util", "Ami/Misc", etc. If you
do  NOT  have your go keys arranged like this, you can still use UllaGulla,
but creating the configfile will be a pain in the butt... more below. It is
NOT  important  how  the  go  keywords  look  like.  For the above example,
"Ami.Demo",  "Ami.Util",  "Ami.Misc"  would  work  just  as  fine. Its only
important that they all start with "Ami", no matter what follows. Expressed
as an AmigaDOS pattern, it would be e.g. "Ami#?"

The  configfile  itself is built up very simple. Each entry consists of two
lines:  the  first  is  the  beginning part of the go keyword (as explained
above),  the  second  is  the  *exact*  go  keyword  of the upload area for
subboards  that match the first line. For the above example, a config entry
of...

    ami
    ami/up

...would  work  perfectly well. It means: all uploads in any subboard whose
go  key starts with "ami" (first line) would go to the subboard with the go
key "ami/up" (second line). An entry of...

    whiskas
    mods/up
    heatbeat
    mods/up

...would  mean  that  uploads  to  any  subboard  whose  go key starts with
"whiskas" (btw. check out his great mods!) would go the subboard "mods/up",
and "heatbeat" would also go to "mods/up".

The  second  example  shows what happens if you don't have your go keywords
arranged  to reflect your directory structure. If the directories "Whiskas"
and  "Heatbeat"  had  go keywords which start with "mods/artists/", as they
both  were in this subdirectory, you would only require ONE entry "mods" to
match both the subboards.

Now,  if  you  have  define  config entries for each and every subboard you
have,  it  will  slow up things, since I have to read the entire config and
parse  it.  The  smaller  the  config,  the faster and almost "unnoticable"
UllaGulla will operate.

You dig? I hope so.

Sorry for my lengthy explanations! :)

I  know  this  could  be  largely extended, it could be made really clever,
subboard  group definitions for one upload subboard, real pattern matching,
etc.  etc. - in fact, I'm too lazy to do that. UllaGulla works fine for me.
It might work fine for you, too - thats why I released it to the public. :)


